/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  ammonia01_synthesis_loop -- the classic Haber-Bosch loop, recycle + purge
  =======================================================================
        makeup (N2:H2 = 1:3 + trace Ar) ─┐
                                         ▼
                                    [mixer] ◄──────── recycle ◄──────┐
                                         │                            │
                                         ▼                            │
                        [converter]  gibbsReactor (SRK)               │
                        N2 + 3H2 <-> 2NH3, 700 K, 200 bar             │
                        (fugacity-corrected equilibrium ~30% NH3)     │
                                         ▼                            │
                        [separator]  isothermalFlash 250 K, 200 bar   │
                          │      │   (chill: NH3 condenses out)        │
                rawLiquid─┘      └── unreactedGas                      │
                    │                    │                            │
                    ▼               [purgeSplit] splitter             │
              [letdown]              │        │                       │
              flash 20 bar    purge ─┘        └── recycle ────────────┘
                │       │      (bleeds Ar)     (~95% back to the mixer)
      productNH3┘       └letdownGas
      (97.8% NH3)        (H2-rich)

  THE LOOP.  Per-pass conversion is equilibrium-limited (~30% NH3 at the reactor
  outlet), but the RECYCLE lifts overall N2/H2 utilisation high -- the reason the
  loop exists.  The reactor equilibrium AND the separator both use SRK FUGACITY
  (real gas at 200 bar): switch the package to idealGas and the conversion falls.
  Mass closes to 100% on N, H and Ar.

  THE PURGE.  Ar enters with the makeup and would accumulate without end; the
  purge is what makes the loop finite, and Ar reaches steady state
  (Ar_in_makeup = Ar_out, closing at 100.00 %).  But the purge is NOT the
  exit that carries most of it out.  Measured from this case's own
  converged/ streams: of 0.01 kmol/h of Ar in, the purge takes 16.2 %, the
  let-down gas 9.6 %, and 74.1 % leaves DISSOLVED IN THE LIQUID AMMONIA
  PRODUCT -- argon is 1.56 % of that product.  This paragraph used to say
  the purge bleeds it, which the diagram makes easy to believe and the
  streams do not support.  Delete the purge and the recycle still fills
  with Ar until the loop chokes -- try it.

  THE LET-DOWN.  At 200 bar the separator leaves N2/H2 DISSOLVED in the
  liquid (a real high-pressure effect, over-stated here because there is no
  N2/H2-NH3 binary interaction parameter -- SRK over-predicts light-gas
  solubility).  Measured from converged/: the raw separator liquid is
  97.676 % NH3 (1.752 % Ar, 0.385 % H2, 0.187 % N2), and the let-down drum
  (flash to 20 bar) boils the dissolved gas off to give 98.390 % NH3 -- a
  gain of 0.7 percentage points, almost all of it H2 and N2.  These two
  numbers read `~80%` and `97.8%` until 2026-09-24; the first was wrong by
  17 points and made the let-down look like the main separation.  What it
  does NOT remove is the argon, which stays at 1.56 % of the product.

  HONEST SIMPLIFICATIONS.  (1) The loop is isobaric at 200 bar; the makeup
  compression and the feed/effluent heat integration are folded into the unit
  duties (reported), not drawn as separate machines.  (2) The let-down gas
  (letdownGas, H2-rich) is RECOMPRESSED AND RECYCLED in a real plant; here it
  exits as a secondary stream to keep ONE clean recycle tear -- so the H2 loss
  reads higher than industrial.  Recovering it (a recompressor + a second tear)
  is the efficiency refinement.  The topology keeps the loop's ESSENCE: reaction,
  separation, recycle, purge, product finishing.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only
units
(
    {
        name     mixer;
        type     mixer;
        inputs   ( makeup  recycle );
        outputs  ( mixed );
    }
    {
        name     converter;
        type     gibbsReactor;
        in       mixed;
        outputs  ( reacted );
        operation
        {
            T 700.0 K;  P 200.0 bar;
            elements ( N  H  Ar );
            species
            (
                { name N2;  atoms ( 2 0 0 ); }
                { name H2;  atoms ( 0 2 0 ); }
                { name NH3; atoms ( 1 3 0 ); }
                { name Ar;  atoms ( 0 0 1 ); }
            );
        }
    }
    {
        name     separator;
        type     isothermalFlash;
        in       reacted;
        outputs  ( rawLiquid  unreactedGas );
        operation { T 250.0 K;  P 200.0 bar; }
    }
    {
        name     letdown;
        type     isothermalFlash;
        in       rawLiquid;
        outputs  ( productNH3  letdownGas );
        operation { T 250.0 K;  P 20.0 bar; }   // let-down drum: flash off the dissolved N2/H2
    }
    {
        name     purgeSplit;
        type     splitter;
        in       unreactedGas;
        outputs  ( purge  recycle );
        operation { fractions ( 0.05  0.95 ); }
    }
);
